module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.admitted_reclaim_charged_by_first_touch

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data admitted_reclaim_charged_by_first_touch: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "admitted_reclaim_charged_by_first_touch" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a per-subject budget charges a shared, run-determined cost to a subject whose own work did not incur it** INVALID STATE: a per-claim CPU ceiling (in `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms` UNTIL 2026-09-12, when it was deleted there rather than repaired -- see the receipts below; STILL LIVE on the fast lane and in `v2.workflow.floor_enrolment_margin`) is applied to a claim whose measured CPU includes cost that is a property of the RUN -- its memory pressure and its scheduling -- and not of the claim. WHAT THIS CLASS MODELS IS EXACTLY THAT: a reading that is not a function of the subject deciding a per-subject budget. WHETHER ANY PARTICULAR SPECIMEN BELOW CAUSALLY ESTABLISHES ITS OWN ACCOUNT IS A SEPARATE QUESTION AND IS ANSWERED PER SPECIMEN, NOT BY THIS SENTENCE -- changing the class definition does not promote a proposed instance into an established explanation. HOW THE RUN'S COST IS ATTRIBUTED IS NOT ESTABLISHED, AND THIS SENTENCE NO LONGER ASSERTS IT. An earlier spelling of this row said the cost is a property of the run's memory pressure AND OF TOUCH ORDER, and named first-touch as the mechanism; the two-run specimen it was written from was consistent with that. The seven-run series appended later is evidence AGAINST touch order for the affected_set_universe module -- in the SIX runs where both identities produced a reading, both are expensive together or cheap together, which is evidence against one particular first-touch model -- a single shared charge paid once, with a second access to already-warm state therefore cheap in that same run -- rather than against every possible order effect, since no controlled order intervention was performed. Neither reading is asserted here as the mechanism. NOTE ON THE IDENTITY, recorded rather than quietly left: `admitted_reclaim_charged_by_first_touch` names one hypothesis about the attribution rather than the invalid state, so the name is now wider than what the row establishes. Whether it should be renamed is a question for this class's owner and is deliberately not answered by the change that surfaced it -- renaming a landed class row moves the roster import, every citation, and the identity a reader searches for.",

    "HARM: which claim is billed is not determined by that claim's own work, so the ceiling refuses an arbitrary subject; PRs red at random on a fold that is progressing correctly; and the reading cannot be used to rank claims by cost because it does not measure the claim.",

    "THE DISTINGUISHING FACT THAT MAKES THIS A CLASS AND NOT AN INCIDENT: `gunbc.memory_stall_refusal` DELIBERATELY ADMITS running under reclaim. Its refusal is a conjunction of fault RATE and a cpu-share floor precisely so that slow-but-progressing passes, and `memory_stall_admitted_under_pressure_receipt_note` says so in terms, naming this runner class. The system knowingly runs at the high watermark. What is unmodelled is that the ADMITTED cost is then charged to PER-CLAIM CEILINGS AT ALL. How it is attributed among the claims of a run is a separate and open question -- an earlier spelling of this sentence said by first-touch order, and the seven-run series below is evidence against that for the affected_set_universe module -- but the defect does not depend on the answer: a run-level cost inhabiting a per-subject budget is the invalid state under every attribution. THE ADMISSION IS CORRECT AND IS NOT THE DEFECT; the defect is that a cost one module deliberately tolerates is silently billed to a different module's budget.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, AND IT IS THE QUESTION THAT SEPARATES THIS FROM THE SHARED-MEMO FIRST-TOUCHER: IS ANYTHING BUILT. The neighbouring class (`claim_forced_and_unbracketed`, and the unlanded first-toucher notes that name a shared MEMO FILL billed to the first reader) has a miss path: something is computed on first touch, and `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` can bracket that fill with a built-by-preparation receipt. THIS class has NO miss path. Strict-preparation already materialised TypedModule.items, NewlineIndex and char_codes, so nothing is computed on first touch and none of the existing `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` sites can bracket it. A proposed repair that reused those brackets was refuted on exactly this ground. If a first-touch interval is a fill of an artifact that did not yet exist, it is the neighbour; if the bytes already exist and the interval is page reclaim, it is this row.",

    "SPECIMEN, all from gunbc PR 11178 (instrument, unlanded, draft) runs 34701185130 and 34703550173 on srv3-01. Subject identity `v2.test.claim.affected_set_universe.affected_set_universe_gate_processes_match_declared_gates`. The SAME scan, back-to-back inside one claim with nothing between: lookup 427769us then repeat 23170us -- 18.5x. Warm rows flat at 1.0x (23876/23186, 23346/23142). Cold cost is per ITEM TOUCHED (~31us/item; three independent readings agree), split index 7% / text 42% / Node objects 51% -- one page-reclaim effect over three memory regions, not three causes. Floor heartbeat at the time: cgroup memory.high=16106127360 (15 GiB) with the charge at 14.9 GiB through claim-evaluation-fold; cgroup events high 5724 -> 10054; process major-faults 315k -> 597k; stall rates 45665 and 182726 faults/min around the subject claim's 16:19:29; host swap-in in step; user-cpu share falling to 89% then 64%. CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID is user+sys, so refault lands on the claim's ceiling with BYTE-IDENTICAL eval_steps (2884 across all runs).",

    "REPAIRS ALREADY RETIRED BY MEASUREMENT, stated so nobody re-proposes them: marshalling is not the cost (<=181us); memoising the search cannot help a claim with calls=1; a registry-filtered or narrowed scan does not address a reclaim effect and narrowing also changes scope semantics; a pre-warm touch loop in preparation cannot hold because pages evicted under memory.high are evicted again; bracketing the scan out of the ceiling would exempt ~24ms of real warm work and is a DESIGN section 5 escape hatch.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The measured interval is a real CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID reading, not a fabricated number; the fail-stop is a typed interrupt. What is not contained is WHICH SUBJECT the interrupt names: the ceiling is a total operation over a misattributed quantity. Naming this outside the ladder would be the fabricated-plausible-output class, and that class requires a figure that is not a measurement of anything; here the figure measures the thread, including admitted reclaim. Naming it mechanically preventable would be inflation: nothing currently refuses the misattribution, and safety depends on review noticing that a 18.5x cold/warm split is reclaim rather than the claim.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: mechanically preventable, and not higher. The kernel bills a refault to the faulting thread; that is not a constructor the substrate can delete. A structurally-guaranteed or structurally-impossible claim inside reflection would be inflation. The subject of the pressure -- the floor's resident set against memory.high -- is owned by whoever owns the floor's memory budget, and no repair inside reflection addresses it. What this billing class can climb to is a construction in which a per-claim ceiling cannot be applied to an interval that includes reclaim `gunbc.memory_stall_refusal` has already classified as admitted.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, naming the CAPABILITY: per-claim CPU ceilings consume a reading from which admitted reclaim has already been attributed to the RUN -- the subject `memory_stall_refusal` already names -- sufficient that NO RUN-LEVEL COST CAN INHABIT THE CLAIM'S CEILING UNDER ANY ATTRIBUTION -- stated that way deliberately, because a trigger phrased as 'a first-touch refault cannot inhabit the ceiling' would be satisfied by a mechanism that fixed first-touch attribution alone while a run-level charge attributed some other way went on refusing an arbitrary subject, which is DESIGN section 4b(3)'s trigger-narrower-than-the-capability failure. A trigger that names `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu`, a pre-warm loop, a narrowed scan, or an exemption of the warm 24ms does not discharge this: those are artifacts that would contribute to a different class, and measurement has already retired them here.",

    "RECEIPT 2026-09-13, SCOPED TO A TESTED HEAD AND NOT TO main: gunbc PR 11195 REACHED THIS ROW'S SPECIMEN AND DID NOT FIRE ITS TRIGGER. 11195 IS OPEN, DRAFT AND UNMERGED as of 2026-09-13; its tested head is adc6b7d6de47. EVERY BEHAVIOURAL CLAIM BELOW IS ABOUT THAT HEAD, NOT ABOUT THE TREE. At that head the required floor's ceiling gates on `eval_steps` with CPU observed-only, and it reached `affected_set_universe_gate_processes_match_declared_gates` -- this row's own specimen, at byte-identical eval_steps whose CPU tracked memory pressure -- from the other end: a first-touch refault cannot inhabit that head's claim budget, because eval_steps is a property of the tree and not a quantity the kernel can bill a refault to. MAIN STILL ENFORCES THE CPU DEADLINE. `v2.workflow.required_floor` maps `OrdinaryChangedWitnessCostPolicy` to `CpuDeadlineArmed`, so until 11195 lands every ordinary claim on main is still charged the refault this row describes, and this receipt asserts nothing to the contrary. EVEN AT THAT HEAD IT IS DISCHARGE BY REMOVAL RATHER THAN BY THE ATTRIBUTION THIS TRIGGER NAMES: nothing anywhere can yet say how much of a claim's CPU was refault, so the capability is absent and that ceiling is immune by not looking. It is also not corpus-wide at that head -- the fast lane still caps on CPU, and `v2.workflow.floor_enrolment_margin` STILL READS A CPU MEASUREMENT: at adc6b7d6de47 `floor_enrolment_margin_budget_ms` derives its admission budget by applying `implied_clean_run_budget` to `required_floor_per_subject_cpu_line_ms` -- the 500ms line kept in CPU milliseconds precisely because this budget is compared to a CPU reading, the work envelope being a different quantity that shares the magnitude -- and then admits an author against a CPU figure. That module states the divergence rather than smuggling it (DESIGN section 3b): it is deliberate, because enrolment is a ONCE-PER-AUTHOR decision against a multi-run distribution with an inflation envelope applied, not a per-run verdict over a single reading, and whether an enrolment gate should be denominated in steps as well is recorded there as a LIVE QUESTION NOT ANSWERED. So this row does not claim the enrolment path is defective; it records only that the CPU-denominated surface survives at that head, which is what keeps the trigger's PLURAL unsatisfied. The trigger reads 'per-claim CPU ceilings' in the PLURAL; 11195 changes one, and firing on that would be the section 4b(3) grain mismatch inverted, leaving this row cited as coverage for two ceilings it does not cover. DISPOSITION, adjudicated with eager-raven-113 and fierce-lark-661: STAY AS IS -- no rung move, no partial fire, trigger not discharged, row not retired. The capability-grain restatement happens ONCE, when the enrolment margin and the fast lane are both denominated in eval_steps; that follow-up is next in lane after 11195.",

    "UNATTRIBUTED COST CROSSINGS -- CANDIDATE INSTANCES OF THIS CLASS PENDING ATTRIBUTION, 2026-09-13. These are logged because the class had no recorded crossings at all, and they are NOT labelled instances of it: what is established is that each crossing HAPPENED on a PR whose diff does not touch the refusing claim, which makes it a CANDIDATE for this class and nothing more. (i) gunbc PR 11191, run 34723754950 attempt 1, head 49c6d892778165fc360f3b3fbe63b78fb5707454, identity `affected_set_universe_includes_meta_self`, cpu budget 500ms EXCEEDED, cost=551ms, cause=completed_over_cost_requirement, claims_failed=0, interrupted_before_verdict=0, blockers=1. (ii) gunbc PR 11097, run 34728156829, head 2efd4d44034c6b0c52463b4fd544ac6b6c4c788f, identity `affected_set_universe_gate_processes_match_declared_gates`, cost=543ms. WHAT WOULD ATTRIBUTE THEM, AND IT IS THE CAPABILITY THIS ROW'S TRIGGER ALREADY NAMES: a PER-ATTEMPT refault reading on the thread clock for the attempt that refused. Absent that, nothing here identifies the cause of either crossing. THREE THINGS THAT DO NOT SUPPLY IT, each of which was tried and rejected. An unchanged-tree RE-RUN green is a DRAW under the standing no-rerun rule -- it does not clear a completed-over-cost refusal, predeclaring it does not waive the rule, and #11191 was refused at disposition on exactly that ground. SIBLING PRs passing the same gate establish nothing about the refusing identity on the refusing attempt. And MEASUREMENTS FROM ANOTHER ATTEMPT cannot be carried across -- the cold-versus-warm differential elsewhere in this row is claim-local to ITS attempt, and is what argues the mechanism in general rather than the cause of these two. Constant `eval_steps` across crossings shows only that the CPU observation and the step observation are non-interchangeable; equal step counts permit unequal work inside primitives, allocation, cache or context state, or pressure, so it excludes no diff.",

    "UNCERTAINTIES CARRIED RATHER THAN ROUNDED OFF. Two runs on ONE runner (srv3-01). The 'neither' 51% bucket includes ~31ms of unsubtracted instrument clock reads. The answering module sitting last in scope precedence (reported as 437/438, 1093/1094) is an observation 2-of-2 whose mechanism was explicitly retracted as unverified inference; this row does not revive that mechanism.",
    "A SEVEN-RUN SERIES ON THIS ROW'S OWN SPECIMEN MODULE, WHICH EXTENDS THE POPULATION AND WITHDRAWS AN ATTRIBUTION THE ROW USED TO ASSERT. The uncertainty this row already carries -- two runs on one runner -- is answered by replication across seven runs on the required floor, all reading the same two identities of `v2.test.claim.affected_set_universe`. SIX of the seven carry a reading for BOTH identities; the seventh contributes only includes_meta_self, because gate_processes was declined_cost_debt and not executed there, so a missing execution is not a measured reading. Runner identities are NOT transcribed, so this expands RUN coverage and does not establish expanded RUNNER coverage -- the two-runs-on-one-runner uncertainty above is narrowed, not discharged. Read as cpu ms / eval_steps / wall ms, gate_processes_match_declared_gates then includes_meta_self: run 34744074782 head f90ed290fa6, 24 / 2806 / 28 and 30 / 214 / 30; run 34743831699 head da9cdfd0bd4, 49 / 2884 / 98 and 55 / 235 / 97; run 34744219677 head edd1961cb5d, 383 / 2806 / 1835 and 344 / 214 / 1517; run 34743785983 head 531be9ce4c5, 403 / 100 / 1857 and 355 / 100 / 1473; run 34743593682 head 2e4775dd775, 449 / 2806 / 3422 and 405 / 214 / 3414; run 34742869116 head 76a662421f68, 510 / 2884 / 5028 and 457 / 235 / 4568; run 34741745548 head f3e3ec32b1d, gate_processes declined_cost_debt and includes_meta_self 1083 / 235 / 10960. THE FIGURES ARE TRANSCRIBED RATHER THAN NAMED BECAUSE THEIR PRODUCER EXPIRES: they are the observed_cpu_ms, eval_steps and observed_wall_ms columns of each run's `required-floor-claim-cost` artifact, and GitHub Actions artifacts are retained fourteen days, so these are unreadable after 2026-09-27 and the series cannot be re-derived from the runs afterwards.",

    "WHAT THE SERIES ESTABLISHES, GROUPED BY THE COUNTER VALUE ACTUALLY REPORTED RATHER THAN POOLED. 2806 and 2884 are DIFFERENT step counts and 214 and 235 are different step counts, so the endpoints must not be compared across groups. Within equal reported eval_steps: gate_processes_match_declared_gates at 2806 steps, n=3, 24-449ms, a ratio of 18.71; at 2884 steps, n=2, 49-510ms, ratio 10.41. includes_meta_self at 214 steps, n=3, 30-405ms, ratio 13.50; at 235 steps, n=3, 55-1083ms, ratio 19.69. These are arithmetic over the transcribed figures and exclude the censored 100 readings. THE TWO FACTS THAT CARRY THE CLASS: 49ms and 510ms at the SAME reported 2884 steps, and 55ms and 1083ms at the SAME reported 235 steps -- each pair straddling the 500ms ceiling. So the ceiling's verdict changes while the reported counter does not. EQUAL eval_steps IS EQUALITY OF THAT COUNTER AND NOT OF ALL UNDERLYING HOST WORK, which is this repository's own standing ruling about map_insert: an n-row insert fold is quadratic host CPU that eval_steps cannot see. The runs are also at different heads, so identical relevant work is not established by identity alone. What this table supports is that the ceiling's verdict is not determined by the reported counter; it is not by itself proof that the remaining variation is independent of all subject work.",

    "HOW THE WITHDRAWAL ABOVE WAS ESTABLISHED: WHAT THE SERIES IS EVIDENCE AGAINST, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. This is the measurement behind the invalid-state sentence's refusal to name an attribution, and it is recorded here rather than only asserted there. In the SIX runs carrying a reading for both, the two unstable identities are expensive together or cheap together. The model this is evidence against is the single-charge one: a shared cost paid once by the first arrival, leaving the second cheap IN THE SAME RUN. No run shows that pattern. It is not evidence against every possible order effect, and no controlled order intervention was performed. The module's other two identities are flat across all seven, and their step counts are carried here so the both-clocks rule holds for them too -- incomplete_fails_closed at 41, 42, 43, 44, 45, 46, 46 ms over 426 or 467 steps (100 on the censored run), and is_complete at 68, 69, 69, 71, 73, 74, 74 ms over 602 or 664 steps (100 on the censored run) -- so the instability is confined to the two identities that reach the shared producer. Whether execution order can discriminate these outcomes is NOT settled by this series: no controlled order intervention was performed, and the two identities' relative order was not varied independently of anything else. This row's own recognition rule separates an established FILL from an established RECLAIM interval, and what the investigated fill path shows is bounded: strict preparation has materialised the items, so nothing is computed on first touch and there is no fill for `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` to bracket. THAT DOES NOT ESTABLISH RECLAIM. The absence of one artifact-fill path does not exhaust the explanations for CPU variation, and the source of that variation is left unresolved here. So what is withdrawn is the ATTRIBUTION, not the class: the variation is observed ACROSS RUNS, which says where the observations differ and not what caused them, and the single-charge touch-order attribution the row used to assert is unsupported by this series. Calling the cost RUN-LEVEL would attribute it away from the claim's own work, which the equal-counter paragraph above expressly says is not established. The invalid-state sentence has been edited to match, so no live sentence in this carrier asserts what this receipt refutes -- appending a refutation beside a surviving claim would have left one fact with two answers in one carrier, which is the DESIGN section 3 meaning fork, and editing a leaf while the refuted root stands is what section 3's replacement-migration rule forbids.",

    "THE MECHANISM IS DELIBERATELY NOT NAMED IN THIS RECEIPT, AND NEITHER IS THE CAUSE OF THE VARIATION. Across the transcribed observations, reported CPU varies substantially, and equal-counter groups contain readings on opposite sides of the 500ms threshold. Within each identity, higher-CPU observations generally also have larger wall-to-cpu ratios. That is a DESCRIPTIVE TIMING ASSOCIATION, not an independent load measurement and not a monotone relation -- 1835/383 is 4.79 and 1857/403 is 4.61, so the ratio decreases while CPU increases, and an earlier spelling of this paragraph presented the sequence as monotone and completed it with a reading belonging to the OTHER identity. These observations do not establish constant total host work, independence from subject work, or a causal mechanism. Environmental contribution remains a HYPOTHESIS. A wall-to-cpu gap does not explain why CPU itself crossed, and an even earlier draft asserted refault-billed-as-cpu from a reading taken on a different subject; both were corrected before landing.",

    "THE UNPERFORMED INVESTIGATION THAT WOULD BEAR ON THE MECHANISM, RECORDED SO THE NEXT READER RUNS IT INSTEAD OF ARGUING. `gunbc.memory_stall_refusal` already prints a major-fault rate when it refuses, so the discriminator is the major-fault figure in the floor logs of the CHEAP runs 34744074782 and 34743831699 against the EXPENSIVE runs 34743593682 and 34742869116: a matched fault-rate association across those four would STRENGTHEN the reclaim hypothesis without establishing it, since naming faults as the explanation also needs the subject, time and charging relationship that ties them to this claim's measured CPU; and failing to observe the association would WEAKEN it without excluding it, since an absent refusal diagnostic is not a measured zero fault rate and coverage would have to be adequate to read the absence as evidence. It is an unperformed investigation with both outcomes left open, not a test that names a mechanism. Those logs share the fourteen-day retention above, so the measurement is available only until 2026-09-27. It has not been performed, and the owner of the memory-stall line is its home.",

    "THIS RECEIPT RETIRES WITH THE DENOMINATOR CHANGE AND DOES NOT OUTLIVE IT. gunbc#11195 moves the ceiling's denominator to eval_steps, which is a function of the claim; once that lands the series above describes a quantity no gate consumes, and this receipt is spent. It must not be carried forward as evidence about a ceiling that no longer reads cpu, and it is not evidence about #11195's own subject, which is the ceiling's blindness rather than the reclaim billing.",

    "ONE READING IN THE SERIES IS CENSORED AND IS USED FOR TWO COLUMNS ONLY. Run 34743785983 reports eval_steps=100 for all four identities of the module, which is a censored or capped count printed in a column named for a count, not the real step count. Its cpu and wall readings are used above; its steps reading is not used for any constant-work comparison. A censored count published as a count is a separate and silent-wrong shape, and it belongs to the instrument that prints it rather than to this class.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.memory_stall_refusal", decl_name: "memory_stall_admitted_under_pressure_receipt_note", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.required_floor", decl_name: "claim_eval_step_budget_for_identity", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.required_floor", decl_name: "claim_cost_basis_standing", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.claim_forced_and_unbracketed", decl_name: "claim_forced_and_unbracketed", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.affected_set_universe", decl_name: "affected_set_universe_gate_processes_match_declared_gates", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
